Title:
Robot Teams that Cooperate to Search, Track, and Explore

Abstract:
Processes of navigating and observing unknown objects and environments are
pervasive in robotics tasks spanning mapping, tracking, inspection, and
cinematography.
Autonomy is also important to application domains such as search and
rescue where autonomous operation is critical to enabling mobile robots to
penetrate through rubble outside communication range with human operators.
This talk will be split into two parts: The first part will focus on methods for
informative planning and active perception for individual robots and for teams;
and the latter part will introduce my ongoing work in the AirLab on multidrone
reconstruction in which we are seeking to build a sort of "DroneOpticStudio."

Active perception tasks such as autonomously mapping a building or tracking
groups of targets often lead to optimization problems that are difficult to
solve (e.g. NP-Hard) and yet highly structured.
Taking advantage of structure can reduce computation costs and simplify these
problems such as by enabling efficient and accurate objective evaluation or
through efficient distributed algorithms with strong suboptimality guarantees.
The methods we will discuss enable individual robots to navigate and observe
unknown environments at high speeds and will enable teams to quickly and
collectively react to information from new observations.

Since starting at the AirLab, I have been working on developing a
"DroneOpticStudio" where teams of flying cameras will coordinate to observe
interactions between groups of people and record video that will be used to
produce a dense 3D reconstructions of those groups and the people's motions.
Tracking those people in real time, planning out view points, safely
coordinating the multi-robot team, and reconstructing scenes are all challenging
problems that our team is working to solve.
I will briefly describe the system we are building before discussing role of
multi-robot coordination in this domain.

